// BUGS #143: a non-`Bool` condition must be a compile error.
//
// Every condition funnels through codegen's `to_i1`, which is
// `trunc i64 %v to i1` — the LOW BIT of the NaN-boxed value. That is correct for
// a real Bool only by accident of the encoding: __val_tag_bool puts the truth
// value in bit 0. For anything else it is arithmetic nonsense, and the failure
// is silent:
//
//     if (42)  { }   // NOT taken — 42 is even
//     if (43)  { }   // taken     — 43 is odd
//     if ("x") { }   // the low bit of a heap address, so it could flip between
//                    // runs as the allocator handed out a different pointer
//
// `0` being falsey was never a truthiness rule anyone implemented — it was
// `0 & 1 == 0`.
//
// The fix is a checker diagnostic, not a change to `to_i1`: once the condition is
// provably Bool, trunc is the correct lowering and stays. Truthiness was
// considered and rejected — it would need a runtime __val_truthy call per
// condition on all four IR bases, and it converts a type error into a silent
// branch, which is the class of defect this entry is about.
//
// Int is used here as the plainest case. The other constructs that reach the
// same lowering each get their own file, because one file can only prove that
// *something* was rejected — with several bad conditions in one program, four of
// the five could regress silently while the file still failed to compile.
fun main(): Int {
    if (42) {
        IO.println("taken")
    }
    return 0
}
